Date:     Tue, 5 Jan 93 9:27:25 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Swastikas

The thread about the origin and meaning of swastikas puts me in mind of one
of Robert Anton Wilson's bits of wisdom: "Certainty belongs to he that only
has one encyclopedia."

We have several conflicting accounts already, to which I can add two more:

According to Robert Graves (for what its worth - some credit his work very
little), the swastika originally symbolized the initial spark of flame from
the first fire started by the discoverer of the fire-drill of Sankrit myth,
Pramanthan (not the modern sort of fire-drill, but a bow, a stick, and some
tinder like boy scouts use instead of matches).  The Greek analogue to this
mythical hero is Prometheus.  See the entry on Prometheus in Graves'
The Greek Myths for more.

According to a fascinating documentary called The Occult Origins of the
Third Reich which aired on Proletarian Broadcast System (PBS) some time back,
Swastikas were a popular symbol of power/energy/potency in Germany as far
back as WWI, appearing as belt buckles - both orientations, only one of
which was made official by the Nazis later.

Some American Indians used swastikas, too, I've heard.

While I've no idea how we can reconcile all the contradictory claims about
swastikas, some of them may be explained by their independent development
spontaneously in different cultures.  The real problem appears to be getting
things straight about the history of it from Sanskrit through Nazi Germany.

Max is quite right about the mysticism of the Nazis, even if my understanding
of swastikas does contradict his.  Occult Origins of the Third Reich told
some of how much of the Nazi High Command came from the Thule Society, as
does Brad Linaweaver's historical fiction work, "Moon of Ice," a libertarian
work I found to be one of the few horror stories I've liked.

Apparently the Nazis believed that when their race was originally pure that
it had magical powers which would be restored if purified of Jewry!  The
concept of race-poisoning was about as widespread in pre-Nazi Germany as
the concept of environmental pollution is in ours.  (Horrifying gedanken
experiment: substitute "race" for "environment" or "planet" in random
environmentalist literature.)

Just some tidbits I've picked up from various sources which you may feel
free to spread about if you like them.  And to think that some claim that
the Nazis were rationalists!

Tim Starr - Renaissance Now! - Think Universally, Act Selfishly
starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu

"True greatness consists in the use of a powerful understanding to enlighten
oneself and others." - Francois Marie Arouet Voltaire

