Subject: Re: Swastikas

Someone made mention of the difficulty of explaining transmission
(other than independent evolution) of the swastika symbol from
India to Germany. This really isn't a problem. The swastika
symbol adorns a boat-load of ancient Greek pottery. It can be found
on samples from Cyprus to the Mainland and the Cyclades. It may
even be found in Sicily and Etruria, but I can't confirm this
w/o some digging.

Since it is found on Cyprus, this suggests an easy mode of tranmission
from the East thru Cyprus and Greece and north to Germany. There
may be a problem with dates though. Off the top of my head, I think
the Greek appearance are nearly simultaneous and show no sign of
a west/north progression. The pottery on Cyprus may even be post-
Greek migration there.

It is highly likely that the German use of the swastika has its
direct origins in Greece, whatever its origins there.


