Subject: Re: How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

> Imagine a future where our decendants are so rich and powerful that if
> only a small fraction of them want to restructure the galaxy to make
> it more to their liking, and were unopposed by the rest, then they
> could do it within a few hundred million years.

As another poster has pointed out, Robin is bringing up the Fermi 
paradox, wondering where all the aliens are, and speculating that
humankind may be the first intelligent species since we can see no 
obviously artificial structures in space.

Imho, the economic investment involved in restructuring solar systems
or galaxies would be far less than restructuring the species itself.
In his SF novels, Vernor Vinge has put impediments to Singularity so
as to explain its delay. If, as seems likely to me, singularity arrives
well before intersteller space travel, it may preclude the idea of humans
on massive starships: One ST:TNG style starship might more economically
be replaced by trillions of nano-scale ships carrying (or "being") our
posthuman descendents. Any large-scale galactic engineering might only
be done by advanced intelligent species who had not gone thru singularity.
Such engineering may be considered somewhat crude by a postsingularity
civilization, whose best defense might be to push the offending intelligence
thru the singularity, lessening it's lust for vast amounts of energy and
material resources.

In _Marooned in Real Time_, the bulk of the population had passed thru
singularity, yet remained invisible to the technically advanced people
remaining.  Perhaps singularity is a transcendence to another reality
which may not impact ours in any visible way. I expect we'll find this
out for ourselves long before alien spaceships land in D.C.

